WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.500
Two more steps cover what
the connectors can't.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:06.559
SailPoint's service desk integration
can't raise change requests,

3
00:00:06.559 --> 00:00:07.888
which is the third gap.

4
00:00:07.888 --> 00:00:10.484
So a ServiceNow flow raises a standard,

5
00:00:10.484 --> 00:00:15.188
normal or emergency change from the
request, based on its change class.

6
00:00:15.188 --> 00:00:17.838
The kit plans that flow
for its second pass.

7
00:00:17.838 --> 00:00:22.487
Until then, the approver raises the
change record by hand and links it.

8
00:00:22.487 --> 00:00:25.450
Either way, closure still
waits for the re-read.

9
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.547
Disconnected applications, the
ones with only an admin console,

10
00:00:29.547 --> 00:00:31.788
run in the declared-exception lane.

11
00:00:31.788 --> 00:00:36.273
That means a service desk ticket, a
named approver before the session,

12
00:00:36.273 --> 00:00:40.208
a time-limited PIM activation,
a recorded session, an end date,

13
00:00:40.208 --> 00:00:44.300
and a plan-of-action item to
automate or retire the application.

14
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.150
The danger is the
exception that never ends.

15
00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:49.500
End dates and reviews are what stop it.

16
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.080
The ServiceNow flow chooses
the change type from the class,

17
00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:57.172
so a pre-authorized standard change,
a normal change with approvals,

18
00:00:57.172 --> 00:01:00.890
or an emergency change each
leave the record the class needs.
